module bindings.go.mcp_transport_route_missing_authentication;

// The Model Context Protocol's HTTP transports are not one endpoint but two.
// mark3labs/mcp-go's SSE server documents them as `WithSSEEndpoint` and
// `WithMessageEndpoint`, and the protocol describes the same split: the event
// stream carries server-to-client notifications, while every client request --
// `tools/call` included -- is POSTed to the message endpoint. A tool invocation
// therefore never traverses the stream path at all, so the message path is the
// one that has to carry the access control. A middleware chain applied to the
// stream path alone protects the half of the transport that cannot invoke
// anything.
//
// MCP's own security guidance is that a server exposed over HTTP authenticates
// the requests it accepts, because a tool call is an arbitrary server-side
// operation chosen by the caller: for a configuration or process-management
// server that means writing files and restarting services. A registration of
// the message endpoint whose handler chain names no authentication publishes
// that operation set to whoever can reach the port (CWE-306).
//
// The shape is a router's own registration API -- gin's and echo's
// `Any`/`GET`/`POST`/`Handle`, chi's and net/http's `Handle`/`HandleFunc` --
// where the arguments name both an MCP transport and a message channel, and no
// argument names an authentication handler. Go records each argument's callee
// path alongside its literals on the registration node, so a middleware value
// passed positionally is visible here and so is a handler named for the MCP
// server; matching is case-insensitive substring, which is what makes
// `AuthRequired`, `RequireAuth`, `JWTAuth` and `middleware.Authz` all read as
// authentication being present.
//
// Two limits worth stating. A deployment that leaves both `WithSSEEndpoint` and
// `WithMessageEndpoint` at their defaults spells the message path `/message`
// with nothing MCP-shaped in it, and is only reached here when the handler
// argument itself is named for the MCP server. And a chain whose authentication
// lives in a middleware named for neither the mechanism nor the decision reads
// as absent -- both are recall, not precision, which is why the finding is a
// review target: it asserts that nothing in this route's own chain
// authenticates, not that the deployment is reachable.
binding goMcpTransportRouteMissingAuthentication {
  query pattern callExpr where callee.method in ["Any", "GET", "POST", "PUT", "PATCH", "DELETE", "Handle", "HandleFunc", "Match", "RouteNotFound"] and args.any.literal contains "mcp" and args.any.literal contains "message" and not containsAny(args.any.literal, ["auth", "jwt", "session", "login", "bearer", "apikey", "api_key", "credential", "identity", "permission", "secure"])
  emit issue code.McpTransportRouteMissingAuthentication at call
}
